This repository contains the analysis scripts and input data required to reproduce 
the main figures and tables presented in the manuscript "Early establishment 
of small intestine neuroendocrine tumors".

# --- Operating system and run time 

The code was run on Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS (GNU/Linux 6.8.0-137-generic x86_64).
NanoSeq preprocessing (Step 1B) was performed on CentOS 7 (Bianca, UPPMAX).

All R scripts were executed using R v4.3.3. The required R packages and their respective 
versions are specified in each script. Installation of the tools used in this repository 
requires minimal time, and the code executes a short overall runtime.

# --- Instructions to reproduce the main findings

Note: To skip Steps 1 and 2, use the prefiltered somatic mutations provided in 
the input_data/WGS directory:

  * SNVs.tsv: SNVs for all patients.

  * mutations.tsv: SNVs and indels for all patients.

  * shared_mutations_SNV.rds: Matrix containing the number of overlapping SNVs within each patient.

Input files required for the NanoSeq analysis are provided in the input_data/NanoSeq directory.

# --- 1. Preprocess from FASTQ

A. Whole-genome sequencing (WGS)
The following tools are required for preprocessing. Follow the installation instructions 
provided by the developers of each tool.

  * bwa (v0.7.17-r1188)
  * samtools (v1.22.1)
  * GATK toolkit (v4.1.5.0)
  * VarScan (v2.3.9)
  * VEP (v109)

Preprocessing steps:

  * Align FASTQ files using bwa mem -M.
  * Identify duplicate reads using MarkDuplicates (GATK toolkit) with default settings.
  * Perform base quality score recalibration using BaseRecalibrator followed by ApplyBQSR (GATK toolkit), 
    with default settings.
  * Perform mutation calling using Mutect2 (GATK toolkit) and VarScan with default settings.
  * Annotate filtered mutation calls from Mutect2 using VEP (mutations marked as PASS in the Mutect2 output).

B. Nanorate sequencing (NanoSeq)

Follow the installation instructions and pipeline provided at https://github.com/AlexandrovLab/DupCaller (v1.2.1).

This pipeline generates the default output files, which are also provided in input_data/NanoSeq 
and can be used to reproduce the results.

# --- 2. Filter somatic mutation (WGS)

* Run the script scripts/analysis/filter.R. This requires text files containing 
  unfiltered mutation calls for each sample (generated in Step 1), together with the 
  provided metadata and a panel of normals (PON). The script generates text files 
  containing mutations and SNVs, as well as a matrix of overlapping mutations within 
  each patient. These outputs are used in subsequent analyses.
  
* The PON is generated from germline and LOH calls obtained using VarScan. Variants 
  supported by more than one read in the matched blood normal samples of more than 
  one patient are included in the PON and subsequently excluded from the filtered 
  somatic mutation calls.
  
* The following input files are required to run the script:
    - sample_code.filtered.onlyPASS.vep.tsv: Filtered (PASS only) and VEP-annotated output from Mutect2. 
      (Expected columns: sample_id, chr, pos, ref, alt, tumor_alt_read_depth_r1, tumor_alt_read_depth_r2, 
      tumor_ref_read_depth_r1, tumor_ref_read_depth_r2, normal_alt_read_depth_r1, normal_alt_read_depth_r2, 
      normal_ref_read_depth_r1, normal_ref_read_depth_r2, Consequence, IMPACT, SYMBOL, BIOTYPE, 
      Existing_variation, DISTANCE, SIFT, PolyPhen
    - sample_code.filtered.tsv: Raw output from FilterMutectCalls.
      (Expected columns: sample_id, chr, pos, ref, alt, tumor_alt_read_depth_r1, tumor_alt_read_depth_r2, 
      tumor_ref_read_depth_r1, tumor_ref_read_depth_r2, normal_alt_read_depth_r1, normal_alt_read_depth_r2, 
      normal_ref_read_depth_r1, normal_ref_read_depth_r2)
    - sample_code.indel.Somatic: Unfiltered indel output from Varscan.
      (Expected columns: chrom, position, ref, var, normal_reads1, normal_reads2, normal_var_freq, normal_gt, 
      tumor_reads1, tumor_reads2, tumor_var_freq, tumor_gt, somatic_status, variant_p_value, somatic_p_value, 
      tumor_reads1_plus, tumor_reads1_minus, tumor_reads2_plus, tumor_reads2_minus, normal_reads1_plus, 
      normal_reads1_minus, normal_reads2_plus normal_reads2_minus)
    - sample_code.snp.Somatic: Unfiltered snp output from Varscan.
      (Expected columns: Same as sample_code.indel.Somatic)
    - sample_code.indel.Somatic.hc: Filtered (hc) indel output from Varscan. 
      (Expected columns: Same as sample_code.indel.Somatic)
    - sample_code.snp.Somatic.hc: Filtered (hc) snp output from Varscan. 
      (Expected columns: Same as sample_code.indel.Somatic)
    
  Note: The indel output from VarScan must be parsed to match the reference and alternative allele 
  notation used in Mutect2.
  
# --- 3. WGD analysis

  * Run the script scripts/analysis/WGD.R to generate the file input_data/WGS/WGD.tsv, 
    which is used in scripts/analysis/figure2_figure3_read_data.R.
    
  * The resulting file is already provided at input_data/WGS/WGD.tsv and can be used directly in 
    subsequent analyses.

# --- 4. Signature analysis - SigProfilerAssignment

MatrixGenerator (v1.4.0) and SigProfilerAssignment (v1.1.5) are required for this analysis.

The input files required to generate the figures are provided and can be used directly to reproduce the results.

  1. Run the script scripts/analysis/Signatures/matrixgenerator/prepare_input.R 
     followed by scripts/analysis/Signatures/matrixgenerator/prepare_input_bootstrap.R.
     This will generate 500 input files to MatrixGenerator.
  2. Run the script scripts/analysis/Signatures/matrixgenerator/run_matrix_generator_bootstrap.sh.
     This wil generate 500 input matrices to SigProfilerAssignment.
  3. Run the script scripts/analysis/Signatures/sigprofiler/run?SigProfiler_bootstrap.py to
     to generate the resulting data used to generate Supplementary Figure 5. 

# --- 5. Purity and and coverage analyses

* Purity: Run the script scripts/analysis/PurBayes/run_purbayes.R. The resulting 
  file is already provided at input_data/WGS/purity.tsv and can be used directly 
  in subsequent analyses.
  
* Coverage: Run samtools idxstats and summarize the total read count across chromosomes 
  1–22, X, and Y for each sample. The resulting file is already provided at 
  input_data/WGS/output_idxstats.tsv and can be used directly in subsequent analyses.

# --- 6. Reproduce figures

* Run the scripts figure1.R, figure2.R, and figure3.R to reproduce the main figures 
  related to the genomic analyses presented in the manuscript. These scripts are 
  located in the scripts/main_figures directory. 
  
* Note: The figure3.R script generates phylogenetic trees for all patients 
  (Supplementary Figure 7), rather than only the representative examples shown 
  in Figure 3. As described in Methods, phylogenetic reconstruction from WGS 
  somatic mutation calls was performed with MEGAX (v10.2.6). The code provided
  generate the same phylogenetic trees using R code. 
  
* Run the scripts in the scripts/supp_figures directory to reproduce Supplementary 
  Figures 1–2 and 4–6, as well as the figures in Supplementary Note 1.

# --- 7. Supplementary tables

* Run the script scripts/supplementary_tables.R to reproduce Supplementary 
  tables 1 and 3-7.